Here are a few ways to write a JavaScript function that computes the average of an array of numbers, ranging from a traditional loop to more modern, concise methods using `reduce`.

### 1. Using the `reduce()` method (Recommended - Most concise)

The `reduce()` method is a very idiomatic way in JavaScript to boil an array down to a single value.

```javascript
/**
 * Computes the average of an array of numbers.
 * @param {number[]} numbers - An array of numbers.
 * @returns {number} The average of the numbers, or 0 if the array is empty.
 */
function calculateAverage(numbers) {
  if (!numbers || numbers.length === 0) {
    return 0; // Handle the edge case of an empty or null array
  }

  // 1. Use reduce to sum all the numbers
  const sum = numbers.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => accumulator + currentValue, 0);

  // 2. Calculate the average
  return sum / numbers.length;
}

// Examples:
